            <ArticleTitle>What else is hiding behind superior mesenteric artery syndrome?</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/jscr/rjv057</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">rjv057</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The superior mesenteric artery syndrome (SMAS) is an uncommon condition in children. We describe a case of a 7-year-old boy with SMAS that occurred 3 years after a Deloyers' procedure for subtotal colonic Hirschsprung who was admitted for bilious vomit, abdominal pain and diarrhea due to unrecognized celiac disease. This case emphasize that SMAS in children needs a close medical and surgical follow-up to avoid an underestimation of early clinical signs unrelated to surgery.</AbstractText>
